{
  "schemaVersion": "rcap-grade-a-verdict-ordering-finding/v1",
  "producedBy": "Captain",
  "producedOn": "2026-09-02",
  "question": "The supersession rule makes one verdict current per family by taking the highest lane number. Does that still select the newest read?",
  "rule": "one current verdict per family: the highest-precedence lane wins (factory vf lanes over codex-cloud directories, then higher lane number); superseded rows remain as history with superseded: true",
  "whyItStoppedTracking": "The re-dispatch moved thirty-five families to lanes that had never read them, chosen by reading every lane's rows. Those destinations are frequently lower-numbered than the lane whose stale verdict the family already carried, so the freshest independent read is now often on the lowest number and loses to the oldest.",
  "method": "For every family with two or more vf-lane verdicts carrying a resolvable verifiedAtBase, the base commit's author time was read from git and compared with what the lane-number rule selects. Recency here is a fact about commits, not an assumption about lane numbering.",
  "familiesMeasured": 179,
  "familiesWhereTheRuleAndRecencyDisagree": 31,
  "ofThoseWhereTheRuleIsMorePermissive": 5,
  "noneIsProvenToday": true,
  "whyThatMatters": "A rule that can carry a stale pass over a fresh failure would be a safety defect. Today it does not: of the families where the rule is the more permissive of the two, not one is COMPLETE_PACKET_PROVEN -- the separate lapse detector catches the byte change and lands them in VERIFY_PENDING or FAIL_REPAIR_REQUIRED anyway. So the live cost is correctness and throughput, not a deliverable shipping unsafely.",
  "theThroughputCost": "Families whose newest independent read is a complete pass are carried as failures from an older, higher-numbered lane, which sends repair lanes to families already repaired.",
  "whatWasNotDone": "The ordering was not changed. Re-ordering would move every one of these families' current verdict in a single step, which is the thing the standing instruction warns against: do not invalidate existing verdicts merely because the detector became stronger. The narrow fix is to order by the commit time of verifiedAtBase and fall back to lane number only where no base resolves.",
